<subsection xml:id="subsec-homogeneous-higher-order">
  <title>Homogeneous Linear ODEs with Constant Coefficients</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      We now develop a complete solution procedure for homogeneous linear ODEs
      with constant coefficients:
      <me>
        y^{(n)}(t) + a_{n-1}\, y^{(n-1)}(t) + \cdots + a_1\, y'(t) + a_0\, y(t) = 0.
      </me>
      The key idea is that exponential trial functions <m>y(t) = e^{\lambda t}</m>
      lead naturally to a polynomial in <m>\lambda</m> — the <em>characteristic
      polynomial</em>.  Its roots determine the entire solution space.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ─── Motivating case study ─── -->

  <example xml:id="ex-motivating-case-study">
    <title>Motivating Case Study</title>
    <p>
      Consider the homogeneous third-order ODE
      <me>
        y'''(t) - 3y''(t) + 4y'(t) - 2y(t) = 0.
      </me>
      We guess a solution of the form <m>y(t) = e^{\lambda t}</m>.
      Computing derivatives: <m>y' = \lambda e^{\lambda t}</m>,
      <m>y'' = \lambda^2 e^{\lambda t}</m>, <m>y''' = \lambda^3 e^{\lambda t}</m>.
      Substituting into the ODE and dividing by <m>e^{\lambda t} \neq 0</m>:
      <me>
        \lambda^3 - 3\lambda^2 + 4\lambda - 2 = 0.
      </me>
      This algebraic equation — the characteristic polynomial evaluated at
      <m>\lambda</m> — must be satisfied.  Once we find its roots, we can
      read off the solutions to the ODE.
    </p>
  </example>

  <!-- ─── Characteristic polynomial ─── -->

  <definition xml:id="def-characteristic-polynomial">
    <title>Characteristic Polynomial</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The <term>characteristic polynomial</term> of the homogeneous linear
        ODE with constant coefficients
        <me>
          y^{(n)}(t) + a_{n-1}\, y^{(n-1)}(t) + \cdots + a_1\, y'(t)
          + a_0\, y(t) = 0
        </me>
        is the degree-<m>n</m> polynomial
        <me>
          P(\lambda) = \lambda^n + a_{n-1}\, \lambda^{n-1}
          + \cdots + a_1\, \lambda + a_0.
        </me>
        The ODE and its characteristic polynomial are obtained from each other
        by the formal substitution <m>y^{(k)}(t) \;\longleftrightarrow\; \lambda^k</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <remark xml:id="rem-fundamental-theorem">
    <title>Counting the Roots</title>
    <p>
      By the fundamental theorem of algebra, every polynomial of degree
      <m>n</m> over the complex numbers has exactly <m>n</m> roots counted with
      multiplicity.
      Each root of <m>P</m> contributes one or more linearly independent
      solutions to the ODE, and together they span the entire solution space
      (which has dimension <m>n</m>).
    </p>
  </remark>

  <p xml:id="par-complex-roots-real-solutions">
      <em>Why complex roots produce sine and cosine.</em>
      Even when the ODE has real coefficients, it is useful to temporarily
      allow complex-valued trial solutions.  If
      <m>\lambda=\alpha+i\beta</m> is a complex root, then
      <me>
        e^{\lambda t}
        = e^{(\alpha+i\beta)t}
        = e^{\alpha t}\bigl(\cos(\beta t)+i\sin(\beta t)\bigr).
      </me>
      The real part <m>e^{\alpha t}\cos(\beta t)</m> and the imaginary part
      <m>e^{\alpha t}\sin(\beta t)</m> are real-valued solutions of the same
      homogeneous ODE.  This is why oscillating sine and cosine terms appear
      whenever the characteristic polynomial has a complex conjugate pair of
      roots.
  </p>

  <!-- ─── Main theorem: roots → solutions ─── -->

  <theorem xml:id="thm-solutions-from-roots">
    <title>Solutions from the Roots of the Characteristic Polynomial</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>P(\lambda)</m> be the characteristic polynomial of a
        homogeneous linear ODE with constant coefficients, and let
        <m>\lambda_0</m> be a root of <m>P</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Case 1 — simple real root.</em>
        If <m>\lambda_0 \in \R</m> is a simple root, then
        <me>
          y(t) = e^{\lambda_0 t}
        </me>
        is a solution.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Case 2 — simple pair of complex conjugate roots.</em>
        If <m>\lambda_0 = \alpha + i\beta</m> and
        <m>\overline{\lambda_0} = \alpha - i\beta</m>
        (with <m>\alpha, \beta \in \R</m>, <m>\beta \neq 0</m>) are simple roots,
        then the two real solutions
        <me>
          y_1(t) = e^{\alpha t}\cos(\beta t), \qquad
          y_2(t) = e^{\alpha t}\sin(\beta t)
        </me>
        are linearly independent solutions.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Case 3 — root of multiplicity <m>k \geq 2</m>.</em>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <em>Case 3a — real root <m>\lambda_0 \in \R</m> of multiplicity <m>k</m>.</em>
              The <m>k</m> linearly independent solutions are
              <me>
                e^{\lambda_0 t},\quad t\, e^{\lambda_0 t},\quad \ldots,\quad
                t^{k-1} e^{\lambda_0 t}.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <em>Case 3b — complex root <m>\lambda_0 = \alpha + i\beta</m> of multiplicity <m>k</m>.</em>
              Conjugacy forces <m>\overline{\lambda_0}</m> to also be a root of
              multiplicity <m>k</m>.
              The <m>2k</m> linearly independent real solutions are
              <md>
                <mrow>
                  e^{\alpha t}\cos(\beta t),\quad \amp e^{\alpha t}\sin(\beta t),
                </mrow>
                <mrow>
                  t\, e^{\alpha t}\cos(\beta t),\quad \amp t\, e^{\alpha t}\sin(\beta t),
                </mrow>
                <mrow>
                  \vdots\quad\amp\vdots
                </mrow>
                <mrow>
                  t^{k-1} e^{\alpha t}\cos(\beta t),\quad \amp
                  t^{k-1} e^{\alpha t}\sin(\beta t).
                </mrow>
              </md>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <p xml:id="par-repeated-roots-t-factors">
      <em>Where the factors <m>t, t^2, \ldots</m> come from.</em>
      Write the differential operator on the left-hand side as
      <m>L=P(D)</m>, where <m>D</m> means differentiation with respect to
      <m>t</m>.  If <m>\lambda_0</m> is a root of multiplicity <m>k</m>, then
      the characteristic polynomial contains the factor
      <m>(\lambda-\lambda_0)^k</m>.  Correspondingly, the differential
      operator contains the factor <m>(D-\lambda_0)^k</m>.
  </p>
  <p>
      Now observe the first step:
      <me>
        (D-\lambda_0)\bigl(t\,e^{\lambda_0 t}\bigr)
        = e^{\lambda_0 t}.
      </me>
      Applying <m>(D-\lambda_0)</m> a second time gives zero.  More generally,
      each factor <m>t^j e^{\lambda_0 t}</m> is killed after applying
      <m>(D-\lambda_0)</m> exactly <m>j+1</m> times.  Therefore, if the root
      has multiplicity <m>k</m>, the functions
      <m>e^{\lambda_0 t}, t e^{\lambda_0 t}, \ldots,
      t^{k-1}e^{\lambda_0 t}</m> all solve the ODE.
      The powers of <m>t</m> are thus not a trick: they record how many times
      the same root occurs in the characteristic polynomial.
  </p>

  <!-- ─── Roots-to-solutions visual ─── -->

  <figure xml:id="fig-roots-to-solutions">
    <caption>
      Summary: how the type of a root of the characteristic polynomial
      determines the corresponding fundamental solutions.
      The multiplicity <m>k</m> of each root is matched by exactly
      <m>k</m> linearly independent solutions (or <m>2k</m> for complex pairs).
    </caption>
    <image xml:id="image-roots-to-solutions" width="80%">
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}[
          node distance=0.5cm and 0.3cm,
          hdrleft/.style={draw, fill=gray!20, minimum width=3.9cm,
                      text width=3.45cm, minimum height=0.7cm,
                      align=center, font=\small\bfseries, inner sep=4pt},
          hdrright/.style={draw, fill=gray!20, minimum width=5.15cm,
                      text width=4.7cm, minimum height=0.7cm,
                      align=center, font=\small\bfseries, inner sep=4pt},
          cellleft/.style={draw, minimum width=3.9cm, text width=3.45cm,
                       minimum height=1.2cm, align=center,
                       font=\small, inner sep=4pt},
          cellright/.style={draw, minimum width=5.15cm, text width=4.7cm,
                       minimum height=1.2cm, align=center,
                       font=\small, inner sep=4pt},
          cellleftb/.style={draw, minimum width=3.9cm, text width=3.45cm,
                        minimum height=1.95cm, align=center,
                        font=\small, inner sep=4pt},
          cellrightb/.style={draw, minimum width=5.15cm, text width=4.7cm,
                        minimum height=1.95cm, align=center,
                        font=\small, inner sep=4pt}
        ]
          \node[hdrleft] (h1) {Root of \(P(\lambda)\)};
          \node[hdrright, right=0cm of h1] (h2) {Fundamental solution(s)};

          \node[cellleft, below=0cm of h1] (r1)
              {\(\lambda_0 \in \R\), simple};
          \node[cellright, right=0cm of r1] (s1)
              {\(e^{\lambda_0 t}\)};

          \node[cellleft, below=0cm of r1] (r2)
              {\(\lambda_0 = \alpha \pm i\beta\), simple};
          \node[cellright, right=0cm of r2] (s2)
              {\(e^{\alpha t}\!\cos\beta t\),\\ \(e^{\alpha t}\!\sin\beta t\)};

          \node[cellleftb, below=0cm of r2] (r3)
              {\(\lambda_0 \in \R\),\\ multiplicity \(k\)};
          \node[cellrightb, right=0cm of r3] (s3)
              {\(e^{\lambda_0 t},\; t e^{\lambda_0 t},\;\ldots,\; t^{k-1}e^{\lambda_0 t}\)};

          \node[cellleftb, below=0cm of r3] (r4)
              {\(\lambda_0 = \alpha \pm i\beta\),\\ multiplicity \(k\)};
          \node[cellrightb, right=0cm of r4] (s4)
              {\(t^j e^{\alpha t}\!\cos\beta t\),\\ \(t^j e^{\alpha t}\!\sin\beta t\),\\ \(j = 0,1,\ldots,k{-}1\)};
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <p xml:id="par-homogeneous-scaled-sums">
        <em>Principle: adding and scaling homogeneous solutions.</em>
        Suppose <m>y_1(t),\ldots,y_r(t)</m> are solutions of the homogeneous
        linear ODE
        <me>
          y^{(n)} + a_{n-1}y^{(n-1)}+\cdots+a_1y'+a_0y=0.
        </me>
        Choose real numbers <m>C_1,\ldots,C_r</m>, multiply each solution
        <m>y_j</m> by its chosen number <m>C_j</m>, and add the results:
        <me>
          y(t)=C_1y_1(t)+\cdots+C_ry_r(t).
        </me>
        Then this new function <m>y(t)</m> is again a solution of the same
        homogeneous ODE.  Such a sum is called a <term>linear combination</term>
        of the functions <m>y_1,\ldots,y_r</m>.
  </p>
  <p>
        <em>Proof.</em>
        Let <m>L[y]</m> denote the left-hand side of the ODE:
        <m>L[y]=y^{(n)}+a_{n-1}y^{(n-1)}+\cdots+a_1y'+a_0y</m>.
        Differentiation respects scaling and addition, so
        <me>
          L[C_1y_1+\cdots+C_ry_r]
          = C_1L[y_1]+\cdots+C_rL[y_r].
        </me>
        Since each <m>y_j</m> solves the homogeneous ODE, each
        <m>L[y_j]</m> is zero.  Hence
        <me>
          L[y]=C_1\cdot0+\cdots+C_r\cdot0=0,
        </me>
        so <m>y(t)</m> is again a solution.
  </p>

  <!-- ─── General solution theorem ─── -->

  <theorem xml:id="thm-general-solution-homogeneous">
    <title>General Solution of Homogeneous Linear ODEs</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the homogeneous linear ODE with constant coefficients
        <me>
          y^{(n)}(t)+a_{n-1}y^{(n-1)}(t)+\cdots+a_1y'(t)+a_0y(t)=0,
        </me>
        and let
        <me>
          P(\lambda)=\lambda^n+a_{n-1}\lambda^{n-1}
          +\cdots+a_1\lambda+a_0
        </me>
        be its characteristic polynomial.
        Applying <xref ref="thm-solutions-from-roots"/> to all roots of
        <m>P</m>, counted with multiplicity, yields <m>n</m> linearly
        independent solutions <m>y_1(t), \ldots, y_n(t)</m>.
        The <term>general solution</term> is
        <me>
          y(t) = C_1\, y_1(t) + C_2\, y_2(t) + \cdots + C_n\, y_n(t),
          \qquad C_1, \ldots, C_n \in \R.
        </me>
        Initial conditions (e.g. values of <m>y(t_0), y'(t_0), \ldots,
        y^{(n-1)}(t_0)</m>) pin down the constants <m>C_i</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <!-- ─── Worked examples ─── -->

  <example xml:id="ex-homogeneous-double-real-root">
    <title>Homogeneous ODE — Double Real Root</title>
    <p>
      Find the general solution of <m>y''(t) - 2y'(t) + y(t) = 0</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Solution.</em>
      The characteristic polynomial is
      <me>
        P(\lambda) = \lambda^2 - 2\lambda + 1 = (\lambda - 1)^2.
      </me>
      The root <m>\lambda_0 = 1</m> has multiplicity <m>2</m> (Case 3a).
      The two fundamental solutions are
      <me>
        y_1(t) = e^t, \qquad y_2(t) = t\, e^t.
      </me>
      The general solution is
      <me>
        y(t) = C_1\, e^t + C_2\, t\, e^t, \qquad C_1, C_2 \in \R.
      </me>
    </p>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="ex-homogeneous-complex-roots">
    <title>Homogeneous ODE — Simple Complex Conjugate Roots</title>
    <p>
      Find the general solution of <m>y''(t) - 2y'(t) + 5y(t) = 0</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Solution.</em>
      The characteristic polynomial is <m>P(\lambda) = \lambda^2 - 2\lambda + 5</m>.
      Using the quadratic formula:
      <me>
        \lambda_{1,2} = \frac{2 \pm \sqrt{4 - 20}}{2} = 1 \pm 2i.
      </me>
      Thus <m>\alpha = 1</m>, <m>\beta = 2</m> (Case 2).
      The two fundamental solutions are
      <me>
        y_1(t) = e^t \cos(2t), \qquad y_2(t) = e^t \sin(2t).
      </me>
      The general solution is
      <me>
        y(t) = e^t\!\left(C_1 \cos(2t) + C_2 \sin(2t)\right),
        \qquad C_1, C_2 \in \R.
      </me>
    </p>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="ex-homogeneous-third-order-mixed">
    <title>Homogeneous ODE of Order 3 — Mixed Roots</title>
    <p>
      Find the general solution of
      <m>y'''(t) - y''(t) + y'(t) - y(t) = 0</m>
      given that <m>\lambda = 1</m> is a root.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Solution.</em>
      The characteristic polynomial is
      <m>P(\lambda) = \lambda^3 - \lambda^2 + \lambda - 1</m>.
      Since <m>\lambda = 1</m> is a root, we factor out <m>(\lambda - 1)</m>
      by polynomial division:
      <me>
        P(\lambda) = (\lambda - 1)(\lambda^2 + 1).
      </me>
      The roots of <m>\lambda^2 + 1 = 0</m> are <m>\lambda_{2,3} = \pm i</m>
      (i.e. <m>\alpha = 0</m>, <m>\beta = 1</m>).
      Applying Cases 1 and 2:
      <me>
        y_1(t) = e^t, \quad y_2(t) = \cos(t), \quad y_3(t) = \sin(t).
      </me>
      The general solution is
      <me>
        y(t) = C_1\, e^t + C_2 \cos(t) + C_3 \sin(t),
        \qquad C_1, C_2, C_3 \in \R.
      </me>
    </p>
  </example>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-homogeneous-higher-order-check">
    <title>Check Your Understanding</title>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-homogeneous-general-solution-from-roots">
      <title>General Solution from Characteristic Roots</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose the characteristic polynomial of a homogeneous linear ODE has the two roots
          <m>\lambda_1=1</m> and <m>\lambda_2=-2</m>.
          What is the general solution?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><m>y(t)=C_1e^t+C_2e^{-2t}</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Correct. Each simple real root contributes one exponential solution.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>y(t)=C_1\cos t+C_2\sin(2t)</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. Trigonometric terms arise from complex roots, not from two real roots.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>y(t)=C_1te^t+C_2e^{-2t}</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. The factor <m>t</m> appears only for repeated roots.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>y(t)=C_1e^{t-2t}</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. The two fundamental solutions must appear independently.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-homogeneous-solve-simple-constant-coeff">
      <title>Solve a Simple Homogeneous ODE</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve
          <me>
            y''-3y'+2y=0.
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The characteristic polynomial is
          <me>
            \lambda^2-3\lambda+2=(\lambda-1)(\lambda-2).
          </me>
          So the roots are <m>1</m> and <m>2</m>. Therefore the general solution is
          <me>
            y(t)=C_1e^t+C_2e^{2t},
            \qquad C_1,C_2\in\R.
          </me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>

</subsection>
